=== L1 Extrinsic Factors Affecting Injury Risk ===
id: 5ced2565-ec32-45a3-9e91-aa5f0f798717
slug: lesson-01
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:34:04.876963+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 19

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How type of activity, coaching, environment and equipment shape the risk and severity of sports injuries.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Are Extrinsic Factors?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Not every sports injury can be blamed on the athlete. <dfn class="term" data-def="Factors that exist outside the individual athlete and can increase the risk or severity of a sports injury.">Extrinsic factors</dfn> are influences in the athlete's external environment — the type of sport they play, the quality of coaching they receive, the surface they perform on, and the equipment they wear. Understanding each factor is essential because examiners regularly ask you to apply them to real sporting scenarios and to explain how they interact with one another.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Type of Sporting Activity</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The nature of a sport is the starting point for risk assessment. High-contact sports such as rugby union or ice hockey carry a higher probability of traumatic injuries — fractures, dislocations and concussion — because direct physical collision is a core part of play. Individual precision sports such as golf or archery carry a much lower collision risk but can still produce overuse injuries if technique is poor or training loads are excessive. Water-based activities introduce the unique risk of drowning and hypothermia, while winter sports on snow and ice raise the likelihood of high-speed falls. When comparing activities, consider contact level, speed of movement, use of implements, and the natural environment involved.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Cover this and state two ways in which a high-contact sport differs from a non-contact sport in terms of injury risk. Think about the type of injury, not just the chance of injury.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>High-contact sports (e.g. rugby, ice hockey) are associated with acute traumatic injuries such as fractures and concussion, whereas non-contact or precision sports are more commonly linked to chronic overuse injuries if training loads are poorly managed.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">Coaching, Instruction and Leadership</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n7">A coach is one of the most powerful protective factors in sport — or, when provision is poor, one of the greatest risk factors. Five key elements are identified. <dfn class="term" data-def="A coach's understanding of correct technique, the rules of the sport and relevant safety regulations.">Knowledge of techniques, rules and regulations</dfn> ensures athletes are taught safe movement patterns from the outset. Experience allows a coach to spot early signs of fatigue or poor technique that a novice coach might miss. Clear communication means that instructions about correct warm-up, equipment use and safe landing technique are actually understood. Supervision ensures athletes are never left to practise dangerous skills unsupervised. Finally, ethical standards — including not pressurising athletes to play through pain — protect long-term health. A coach who lacks experience and fails to supervise correctly multiplies injury risk significantly.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Environment</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">The environment an athlete competes or trains in has three dimensions: weather and temperature conditions, the playing surface and surrounding area, and human interaction. Extreme heat increases the risk of heat exhaustion and dehydration; extreme cold raises the likelihood of muscle strains because cold muscles have reduced elasticity. Wet or icy conditions reduce surface traction, dramatically increasing the chance of slips and falls. <dfn class="term" data-def="The type of ground or court on which sport is performed, whether natural (e.g. grass) or artificial (e.g. 3G turf or hardwood).">Playing surface</dfn> matters greatly: a waterlogged grass pitch increases the chance of ankle sprains, while an excessively hard artificial surface increases ground-reaction forces on joints, raising the risk of stress fractures and shin splints. Human interaction — other performers, officials and spectators — also plays a role. Opponents who play dangerously or illegally raise contact injury risk; officials who fail to enforce rules allow dangerous play to continue; hostile crowds can increase performer anxiety, which in turn can lead to reckless play and injury.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How does playing surface link to footwear choices?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">The spec explicitly links playing surface with appropriate footwear as an example of how extrinsic factors interact. A footballer wearing long metal studs on an artificial 3G surface will find the studs catching rather than releasing, placing enormous rotational stress on the knee — increasing the risk of an ACL sprain. Wearing moulded rubber studs designed for artificial turf reduces this risk. Similarly, a cross-country runner wearing road-running shoes on muddy terrain lacks the grip needed to prevent ankle inversion, so appropriate footwear choice is always surface-dependent. In exam answers, linking surface type to footwear selection will consistently earn higher marks.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Equipment</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12"><dfn class="term" data-def="Gear designed to shield the body from impact, collision or environmental hazards during sport, such as shin guards, helmets and gum shields.">Protective equipment</dfn> is specifically designed to reduce both the likelihood and severity of injury. A cyclist wearing a correctly fitted helmet significantly reduces the severity of any head impact; shin guards in football reduce the severity of direct blows to the tibia. Performance equipment — bats, rackets, bikes, poles — must be correctly fitted and in good condition; a cracked hockey stick or a poorly fitted bicycle saddle can alter biomechanics and lead to overuse injury. Clothing appropriate for the conditions (thermal layers in cold, breathable fabrics in heat) helps regulate body temperature and prevents environmental medical conditions. Footwear is arguably the single most important piece of equipment for injury prevention: it must provide appropriate cushioning, support and grip for the specific surface and activity.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n13" data-revision-tip="Write from memory: give one example of how poorly chosen footwear can directly cause a specific injury. Include the injury name and the mechanism.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Protective equipment reduces both injury likelihood and severity — for example, a correctly fitted mouthguard significantly reduces the risk of dental and jaw injuries in contact sports, while appropriate footwear reduces the risk of ankle sprains by providing surface-specific grip and lateral support.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How can officials reduce injury risk through human interaction?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">Officials are part of the human interaction element of the environment. A referee who strictly enforces rules about dangerous tackles, illegal contact or unsafe play directly reduces the chance of traumatic injuries occurring. Conversely, a permissive official who allows high tackles in rugby or dangerous challenges in football creates an environment where athletes are more likely to be injured. The spec asks you to understand that officials and spectators are part of the environment — they are not passive bystanders but active contributors to risk level. Linking official behaviour to coaching and type of activity in extended answers demonstrates the synoptic thinking OCR rewards.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n16">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">The four extrinsic factors — type of sporting activity, coaching/instruction, environment (weather, surface, human interaction), and equipment (protective, performance, clothing, footwear) — all influence injury risk and severity, and they interact with one another.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Coaching quality is both a risk factor and a protective factor: a knowledgeable, experienced coach who communicates well and supervises correctly can significantly reduce the impact of other extrinsic hazards.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">High marks come from linking extrinsic factors together (e.g. surface type determining footwear choice) and applying them to named sporting scenarios rather than describing each factor in isolation.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n15">For 6- and 8-mark questions on extrinsic factors, the highest marks go to answers that show how factors interact — not just list them. For example: an icy playing surface means that footwear with insufficient grip significantly increases fall risk, and a coach without experience who does not recognise these hazards compounds the danger. Always use a named sport or specific scenario as your example, and use the precise spec language: "playing surface", "protective equipment", "coaching/instructing/leading" rather than informal alternatives.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
