=== L2 Intrinsic Factors Affecting Injury Risk ===
id: 4fdc6dfe-2c21-43dd-aeda-fc337593f96d
slug: lesson-02
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:31:46.210247+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 18

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How individual variables and psychological factors within the athlete influence their risk and severity of injury.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Are Intrinsic Factors?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="Factors that originate from within the athlete's own body or mind and can increase the risk or severity of a sports injury.">Intrinsic factors</dfn> are the internal characteristics that the athlete brings to every session — their age, fitness, nutrition, sleep quality, psychological state and injury history. Unlike extrinsic factors, intrinsic factors cannot always be controlled by a coach or facility manager; they require the athlete themselves to take responsibility for their own preparation and wellbeing. Intrinsic factors divide into individual variables and psychological factors.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Individual Variables</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The specification lists ten individual variables. <strong>Gender</strong> influences injury risk because anatomical differences — including hip width, hormonal fluctuations and average muscle mass — mean that female athletes are statistically more susceptible to ACL injuries, while male athletes are more often involved in high-contact traumatic events. <strong>Age</strong> affects risk in both directions: younger athletes have developing bones and growth plates that are vulnerable to fracture and overuse; older athletes have reduced muscle elasticity, slower recovery rates and often longer histories of accumulated joint stress. <strong>Experience</strong> and <dfn class="term" data-def="The correctness of an athlete's movement patterns when performing sports skills, which directly affects the stress placed on muscles, tendons and joints.">technique/ability</dfn> are closely related — poor technique places abnormal stress on muscles, tendons and joints, raising overuse and acute injury risk. <strong>Weight</strong> affects the load placed through joints on every step or landing; excessive body mass increases ground-reaction forces through the knees and hips, raising the risk of chronic conditions such as patellar tendonitis and shin splints.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n5"><strong>Fitness levels</strong> interact with weight — a high fitness level means muscles, tendons and ligaments are better conditioned to withstand the demands of sport. <strong>Nutrition and hydration</strong> directly support muscle repair, bone density and energy availability; a poorly nourished athlete fatigues earlier and is more prone to both acute and chronic injury. <strong>Sleep</strong> is when the body repairs damaged tissue; chronic sleep deprivation impairs reaction time, coordination and pain tolerance, all of which elevate injury risk. <strong>Medical conditions</strong> — such as asthma, diabetes or a known cardiovascular issue — may limit the type or intensity of activity that is safe. Finally, <dfn class="term" data-def="An injury that has occurred before and may not have fully healed, making the same body part more susceptible to re-injury in the future.">previous or recurring injuries</dfn> are one of the strongest predictors of future injury: scar tissue, reduced range of motion and compensatory movement patterns all increase vulnerability.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and list all ten individual variables from memory. Check you have included gender, age, experience, weight, fitness levels, technique/ability, nutrition/hydration, medical conditions, sleep and previous/recurring injuries.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The ten individual variables are: gender, age, experience, weight, fitness levels, technique/ability, nutrition/hydration, medical conditions, sleep, and previous/recurring injuries. Variables can interact — for example, a participant's weight can directly influence their fitness levels and the stress placed through their joints.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Psychological Factors</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">An athlete's mental state is as important as their physical condition. The specification identifies five psychological factors. <dfn class="term" data-def="The internal drive that pushes an athlete to train, compete and push through difficulty; can be intrinsic (from within) or extrinsic (from rewards or others).">Motivation</dfn> affects how hard an athlete trains and whether they take appropriate rest — over-motivated athletes may overtrain, dramatically increasing overuse injury risk. <strong>Arousal</strong> refers to the athlete's level of mental and physical activation; optimal arousal improves performance, but excessive arousal leads to muscular tension, impaired decision-making and reckless movement patterns that increase injury risk. <strong>Anxiety and stress</strong> have physical consequences: elevated cortisol impairs immune function and slows tissue healing; anxiety causes muscle tension that restricts normal movement and alters gait. <strong>Confidence</strong> works both ways — a highly confident athlete may attempt skills beyond their current ability, while a low-confidence athlete may hesitate at a critical moment, exposing them to injury through poor technique or mistimed actions.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Aggression</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10"><strong>Direct aggression</strong> — physical force directed at an opponent outside the rules of the game — is a major cause of traumatic injury, both for the aggressor and the victim. The spec distinguishes this from <strong>channelled aggression</strong>, which is the controlled use of assertiveness within the rules of the sport (e.g. a hard but fair tackle in rugby). Understanding why aggression arises helps coaches manage it. The specification identifies five reasons: <strong>level of performance</strong> (frustration when performing below expectation), <strong>retaliation</strong> (responding to a foul or provocation), <strong>pressures to win</strong> from the performer, coach or spectators, <strong>decisions of officials</strong> (perceived injustice), and <strong>performance-enhancing drugs</strong>, some of which are linked to increased aggressive behaviour.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n11" data-revision-tip="Distinguish direct from channelled aggression in one sentence each. Then list the five reasons for aggression — these are commonly tested in short-answer questions.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Direct aggression (physical force outside the rules) is a cause of injury; channelled aggression (assertiveness within the rules) is not. The five reasons for aggression in sport are: level of performance, retaliation, pressures to win, decisions of officials, and performance-enhancing drugs.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How do mental strategies reduce injury risk?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n12">The specification includes mental strategies as intrinsic tools for managing psychological risk. Mental rehearsal involves the athlete vividly imagining a skill being performed correctly, which reinforces correct movement patterns and reduces the chance of technique breakdown under pressure. Imagery is a broader process of creating and using mental images — including visualising a safe, controlled landing or a measured tackle — to prepare the body and mind for the demands ahead. Selective attention involves filtering out distractions such as crowd noise or crowd-induced anxiety so the athlete can focus entirely on the task, reducing the likelihood of mistimed actions. These strategies are most closely linked to the psychological benefits of a warm up, which is where they are typically applied in practice.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How do individual variables interact with each other?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">The spec explicitly notes that individual variables can influence one another — this is where higher-level exam answers gain marks. For example, an athlete who has poor nutrition and inadequate sleep will also have lower fitness levels and impaired tissue healing — three variables compounding each other. A previous knee injury may lead a footballer to alter their running technique, which in turn places excess stress on the opposite hip, creating a new overuse injury site. Weight can directly influence fitness levels: higher body mass demands more cardiovascular effort during the same exercise intensity, leading to earlier fatigue. In 8-mark evaluate questions, demonstrating that intrinsic factors interact rather than acting in isolation will push an answer into the Mastering band.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n15">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n16">The ten individual variables (gender, age, experience, weight, fitness levels, technique/ability, nutrition/hydration, medical conditions, sleep, previous/recurring injuries) all influence injury risk and can interact with one another in ways that compound risk.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">Psychological factors — motivation, arousal, anxiety/stress, confidence and aggression — are intrinsic risk factors because they directly affect movement quality, decision-making and behaviour during sport.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Mental strategies (mental rehearsal, imagery, selective attention) can reduce psychological injury risk and are closely linked to the psychological benefits of a warm up covered earlier in this unit.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n14">When asked to "compare and contrast" intrinsic factors, structure your answer around both similarities and differences. For example, both poor nutrition and lack of sleep impair tissue repair and increase fatigue — but sleep deprivation additionally impairs reaction time and coordination, which are risk factors unique to that variable. Examiners reward answers that use the precise specification vocabulary ("previous/recurring injuries", "nutrition/hydration", "technique/ability") rather than informal language. For aggression questions, always distinguish direct from channelled aggression — failing to do so typically loses a mark.</p>

