  "marks": "Award up to 4 marks (one per correctly described and exemplified step, maximum 4). Protection — e.g. prevent the performer from weight-bearing, use a support bandage or splint. Rest — e.g. remove the performer from play and stop all activity involving the ankle. Ice — e.g. apply a cold pack wrapped in a cloth for 15–20 minutes every two hours. Compression — e.g. apply a firm bandage around the ankle to limit swelling. Elevation — e.g. raise the ankle above hip height while the performer sits or lies down. [4 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "A rugby player is injured during a match. The physiotherapist runs onto the pitch and begins a SALTAPS assessment. The player is conscious and alert.\n\nDiscuss how the physiotherapist should carry out the SALTAPS assessment and what decisions they might make at each stage.",
  "type": "6 marks — Discuss",
  "marks": "Mastering (5–6 marks): A thorough discussion covering at least four SALTAPS steps with explanation of what the physiotherapist does at each step and what the findings might indicate, including two contrasting outcomes (e.g. if active movement is pain-free the player may continue; if strength testing reveals weakness the player is removed). Some reference to decision-making based on findings.\nSecure (3–4 marks): At least three steps correctly identified and explained with some decision-making discussed.\nDeveloping (2 marks): Two or more steps identified but little explanation of process or decision-making.\nEmerging (1 mark): One relevant point about injury assessment. [6 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "A football coach has two players injured during training: one has a suspected sprained wrist and the other is unconscious on the pitch after a collision.\n\nEvaluate the extent to which having a clear understanding of both SALTAPS and DRABC allows a coach to manage sports injuries effectively and safely.",
  "type": "8 marks — Evaluate (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering (7–8 marks): A balanced evaluation that explains how SALTAPS systematically assesses a conscious injured performer and supports sound return-to-play decisions, and how DRABC provides a life-saving sequence for unconscious or non-breathing performers. The response applies both protocols correctly to the scenario (sprained wrist → SALTAPS then PRICE; unconscious player → DRABC immediately, do not complete SALTAPS). A well-supported judgement is reached — e.g. that both protocols together provide comprehensive first-aid coverage, but that their effectiveness also depends on the coach’s training and calm execution under pressure.\nSecure (5–6 marks): Both protocols described accurately with some applied evaluation and an attempt at a judgement.\nDeveloping (3–4 marks): One protocol covered well, the other only briefly; limited evaluation or judgement.\nEmerging (1–2 marks): Basic first-aid points with no clear evaluation. [8 marks]"
 }
]

