=== L7 Rehabilitation & Reducing the Risk of Injury ===
id: a4be8bca-56ba-4b5b-b3bf-1757aa49a36e
slug: lesson-07
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:44:31.85458+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 21

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Treatments and therapies used in rehabilitation, plus key strategies for reducing the risk of sports injuries.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">From Treatment to Full Recovery</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Once immediate first aid has been given, the focus shifts to <dfn class="term" data-def="The process of restoring a performer's physical function, strength and confidence after injury, enabling a safe return to sport.">rehabilitation</dfn>. Effective rehabilitation is not simply resting until the pain fades — it is a structured programme of treatments and exercises that progressively restore strength, range of movement and confidence. Getting rehabilitation right determines how quickly, and how safely, an athlete returns to their sport.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Treatments and Therapies in Rehabilitation</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">A range of treatments can be used at different stages of the rehabilitation process. <strong>Massage</strong> increases blood flow to the injured tissue, reduces muscle tension and can break down scar tissue during later stages of recovery. <dfn class="term" data-def="A therapy that uses high-frequency sound waves to promote tissue healing, reduce inflammation and relieve pain in soft-tissue injuries.">Ultrasound therapy</dfn> uses sound waves to promote tissue healing and reduce inflammation; it is particularly useful for deep soft-tissue injuries such as ligament sprains.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5"><strong>Electrotherapy</strong> uses electrical stimulation (such as TENS) to reduce pain and stimulate muscle contractions in weakened muscles. <strong>Hydrotherapy</strong> involves exercising in water, where buoyancy reduces the load on injured joints, allowing the performer to rebuild strength and range of movement with less pain. <strong>Cryotherapy</strong> — the therapeutic use of cold — reduces pain, swelling and inflammation; it may be applied as ice packs, ice baths or localised cold-air devices. <strong>Contrast therapy</strong> alternates between cold and warm water immersion to stimulate blood flow, reduce swelling and speed tissue recovery.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><strong>Painkillers</strong> such as ibuprofen (an anti-inflammatory) can reduce pain and swelling, allowing the performer to engage in therapeutic exercises. However, they mask pain signals, so return to activity must be managed carefully. <strong>Support</strong> in the form of kinesiology taping, neoprene sleeves or bandaging provides stability to healing structures and proprioceptive feedback. <strong>Immobilisation</strong> via a cast, splint or sling is used when a bone or severely injured joint must be held still to heal correctly — for example, following a fracture or dislocation.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n7" data-revision-tip="Cover the list and write down as many rehabilitation treatments as you can from memory, then add one benefit for each. Examiners award marks for specific, named treatments not vague terms like 'therapy'.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The main rehabilitation treatments are: massage, ultrasound, electrotherapy, hydrotherapy, cryotherapy, contrast therapy, painkillers (e.g. ibuprofen), kinesiology taping / neoprene / bandaging, and immobilisation (cast / splint / sling). Each is used at different stages and for different injury types.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Psychological Effects of Injury</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Injury does not only affect the body. The psychological impact of being unable to train or compete can be significant. Athletes may experience frustration, anxiety, low mood, reduced confidence and fear of re-injury. During long-term rehabilitation, motivation can drop as progress slows. Mental strategies such as mental rehearsal (imagining performing skills correctly), imagery, and goal-setting are used by sports psychologists and physiotherapists to maintain an athlete's psychological wellbeing and confidence throughout the recovery process.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Stretching as Both Cool Down and Rehabilitation</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">Stretching is not just a cool-down component — it is also a key rehabilitation tool. Maintenance stretches and static stretches applied during rehabilitation restore the normal length of healing muscle fibres, prevent scar tissue contracture and gradually improve range of movement at the injured joint. Proprioceptive Neuromuscular Facilitation (PNF) stretching — where the muscle is contracted and then stretched — is particularly effective in the later stages of rehabilitation to regain full flexibility. This is why warm-up and cool-down stretching techniques are directly linked to injury treatment.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Reducing the Risk of Injury</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Prevention is always better than cure. Several strategies are used before and during sporting participation to reduce the likelihood and severity of injury. <strong>Safety checks and risk assessments</strong> involve systematically identifying hazards in the environment (e.g. wet playing surfaces, damaged equipment) and assessing the level of risk, then implementing control measures to remove or reduce those hazards. Risk assessments also consider the characteristics of the group — age, experience, medical conditions and group size all affect the level of supervision and precautions needed.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13"><strong>Medical screening</strong> identifies pre-existing conditions that could increase injury risk. Athletes may complete health questionnaires, undergo physical examinations or electrocardiograms (ECGs) before contact sport. <dfn class="term" data-def="Policies and minimum standards set by National Governing Bodies to protect participant safety across a sport.">National Governing Body (NGB) policies</dfn> set mandatory safety standards — for example, World Rugby's protocols on concussion management, or the FA's rules on heading in youth football — that all affiliated clubs must follow.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14"><strong>Emergency Action Plans (EAPs)</strong> are written documents that specify in advance: which personnel are responsible for emergency response (emergency personnel), how communication to emergency services will be made (emergency communication), and which equipment such as defibrillators, first aid kits and stretchers is available and where (emergency equipment). An EAP ensures that everyone knows their role the moment an emergency occurs, reducing response time and potential harm.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n15" data-revision-tip="Learn the three components of an Emergency Action Plan (EAP) — personnel, communication, equipment. You could be asked to describe what an EAP should include in a scenario question.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>An Emergency Action Plan (EAP) has three components: emergency personnel (who responds), emergency communication (how to contact services) and emergency equipment (what is available and where). Having an EAP in place means no time is wasted deciding what to do when an emergency occurs.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Mistake: Confusing Cryotherapy and Contrast Therapy</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">Students often confuse cryotherapy (cold only) with contrast therapy (alternating cold and warm). Cryotherapy uses cold alone — ice packs, ice baths or cold-air devices — to reduce acute inflammation and pain. Contrast therapy alternates between cold and warm immersion (for example, 1 minute cold water, 3 minutes warm water, repeated) to stimulate blood flow changes that aid recovery. They are different treatments used at different points in rehabilitation. In an exam, if asked to describe cryotherapy, do not mention warm water.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n18">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Rehabilitation uses a range of named treatments — including massage, ultrasound, hydrotherapy, cryotherapy, contrast therapy, electrotherapy, painkillers, taping and immobilisation — applied at different stages and for different injury types to restore function safely.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Injury also has significant psychological effects including anxiety, loss of confidence and fear of re-injury; mental strategies such as imagery and mental rehearsal support psychological recovery alongside physical treatment.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">The risk of injury is reduced through safety checks and risk assessments, medical screening, NGB policies, and Emergency Action Plans (EAPs) covering personnel, communication and equipment.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n17">For questions about rehabilitation treatments, always name the treatment precisely and state the benefit it provides. Saying 'massage helps the injury' earns no marks. Saying 'massage increases blood flow to the injured tissue, which delivers oxygen and nutrients needed for cell repair' earns full marks. For EAP questions, remember all three components: personnel, communication and equipment — missing one will cost you a mark.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
