=== L8 Common Medical Conditions: Asthma & Diabetes ===
id: e65e641f-37fd-4d4f-b65d-9136c78fcb4e
slug: lesson-08
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:55:01.163101+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Causes, symptoms and management of asthma and diabetes (Type 1, Type 2, hypoglycaemia, hyperglycaemia) in sport.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Medical Conditions in Sport</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Many sports performers live with long-term <dfn class="term" data-def="Conditions that affect normal body function over a prolonged period and may require ongoing management, particularly during physical activity.">medical conditions</dfn>. Asthma and diabetes are two of the most common conditions encountered in sport. Understanding their causes, symptoms and management is essential for coaches, teachers and performers so that participation can continue safely and emergencies can be handled correctly.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Asthma: Causes and Triggers</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Asthma is a chronic respiratory condition in which the airways become inflamed, narrow and produce excess mucus, making breathing difficult. The exact cause of asthma is not fully understood, but two key triggers are especially relevant in sport. <strong>Environmental triggers</strong> include cold air, air pollution, pollen, dust, pet dander and cigarette smoke — all of which can inflame the airways. <strong>Exercise</strong> is a particularly important trigger: during vigorous physical activity, increased ventilation draws cold, dry air deep into the airways, which can provoke an <dfn class="term" data-def="A sudden worsening of asthma symptoms caused by airway narrowing, making it very difficult to breathe.">asthma attack</dfn> — sometimes called exercise-induced bronchoconstriction.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Asthma: Symptoms and Treatment</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">The four key symptoms of asthma are: <strong>coughing</strong> (often worse at night or after exercise), <strong>wheezing</strong> (a whistling or rattling sound on breathing out), <strong>shortness of breath</strong> (difficulty getting enough air, particularly during exertion) and <strong>tightness in the chest</strong> (a squeezing feeling that can feel like pressure). During an asthma attack all four symptoms may occur together and can escalate rapidly.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Treatment in a sporting context has two elements: <strong>reassurance</strong> — remaining calm, helping the performer sit upright in a position that opens the chest, and reassuring them reduces anxiety, which otherwise worsens the attack; and <strong>inhalers or nebulisers</strong> — a reliever inhaler (usually blue) delivers bronchodilator medication (typically salbutamol) that relaxes the airway muscles and opens the airways within minutes. A nebuliser delivers the same medication as a fine mist for more severe cases. If a performer's reliever inhaler does not work after 10 puffs, or if they become very distressed or their lips turn blue, call 999 immediately.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n8" data-revision-tip="Practise listing the four asthma symptoms in order without looking. Examiners commonly ask 'state two symptoms of asthma' — always use the precise NHS terms from the spec: coughing, wheezing, shortness of breath, tightness in the chest.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four symptoms of asthma are coughing, wheezing, shortness of breath and tightness in the chest. Treatment involves reassurance and a reliever inhaler (or nebuliser). If the inhaler fails after 10 puffs, call 999 immediately.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Diabetes: Type 1 and Type 2</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Diabetes is a condition in which the body cannot regulate <dfn class="term" data-def="A hormone produced by the pancreas that allows cells to absorb glucose from the blood and use it for energy.">insulin</dfn> correctly, leading to abnormal blood sugar levels. There are two main types. <strong>Type 1 diabetes</strong> is an autoimmune condition typically diagnosed in childhood or young adulthood — the body's immune system destroys the insulin-producing cells in the pancreas, so the body produces no insulin at all and the person is entirely dependent on insulin injections or a pump. <strong>Type 2 diabetes</strong> is much more common and is strongly linked to lifestyle factors such as a poor diet and physical inactivity; the body either does not produce enough insulin or the cells become resistant to it. Type 2 is more commonly diagnosed in adults, though increasing numbers of young people are affected.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Diabetes: Symptoms and Treatment</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Common symptoms of both Type 1 and Type 2 diabetes include: increased thirst (the body tries to dilute excess blood sugar by drawing water from cells), urinating more often (kidneys filter excess glucose through urine), extreme tiredness (cells cannot access glucose for energy), unexplained weight loss and cuts or wounds that take a long time to heal (high blood sugar impairs immune function and circulation). These symptoms are shared across both types, though Type 1 tends to develop more rapidly.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Treatment differs by type: Type 1 requires daily insulin injections or an insulin pump, as well as careful diet management. Type 2 is often managed initially through lifestyle changes — including increased physical activity and a healthier diet — with medication or insulin added if lifestyle changes are insufficient. Both types require regular blood sugar monitoring, especially around exercise, as physical activity significantly affects blood glucose levels.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n14" data-revision-tip="The key difference in cause between Type 1 and Type 2 is: Type 1 — the body cannot produce insulin at all; Type 2 — the body does not produce enough insulin, or insulin does not work properly. This distinction is often tested in a one-mark question.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Type 1 diabetes: the body cannot produce insulin (autoimmune). Type 2 diabetes: the body does not produce enough insulin or insulin does not work properly (lifestyle-linked). Both types share the same five common symptoms.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Blood Sugar Levels: Hypos and Hyperglycaemia</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">Blood sugar levels in a diabetic performer can fall dangerously low or rise dangerously high, both of which are medical emergencies. <dfn class="term" data-def="A condition where blood sugar falls below the normal range, causing symptoms such as shakiness, sweating, confusion and weakness.">Hypoglycaemia</dfn> (a 'hypo') occurs when blood sugar falls too low — often triggered by exercise, missed meals or too much insulin. Symptoms include shakiness, sweating, dizziness, confusion, pallor and weakness. Treatment is rapid ingestion of fast-acting glucose (e.g. glucose tablets, a sugary drink or orange juice). Exercise significantly increases the risk of a hypo in Type 1 diabetics, so blood sugar should be checked before, during and after activity. <strong>Hyperglycaemia</strong> (high blood sugar) develops more slowly — caused by too little insulin, too many carbohydrates or illness — and symptoms include increased thirst, frequent urination and fatigue. Severe hyperglycaemia requires medical attention.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Managing Asthma During Sport</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">A performer with asthma should always have their reliever inhaler immediately accessible during sport — not locked away in a bag or locker. A thorough warm-up is especially important because it gradually raises ventilation rather than shocking the airways with sudden intense effort. In cold weather, wearing a scarf or sports buff over the mouth and nose warms and humidifies the inhaled air, reducing the exercise-induced trigger. Coaches should know which performers have asthma, where their inhalers are kept, and the action to take if an attack occurs.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Managing Diabetes During Sport</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">A Type 1 diabetic performer should check blood glucose before exercise and have fast-acting carbohydrates (e.g. glucose tablets or a sports drink) immediately to hand during activity. If blood sugar is low before training, a carbohydrate snack should be eaten and glucose rechecked before starting. Exercise generally lowers blood sugar, so insulin doses may need adjusting on training days — this should always be done in consultation with a medical professional. Coaches should know the signs of a hypo and have access to glucose sources at the venue.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Asthma symptoms are coughing, wheezing, shortness of breath and chest tightness; treatment is reassurance plus a reliever inhaler, with 999 called if the inhaler fails after 10 puffs.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Type 1 diabetes means the body cannot produce insulin; Type 2 means it does not produce enough or insulin does not work properly; both share symptoms of increased thirst, frequent urination, extreme tiredness, weight loss and slow-healing cuts.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Hypoglycaemia (low blood sugar) is treated with fast-acting glucose and is a particular risk during exercise; hyperglycaemia (high blood sugar) develops more slowly and requires medical attention if severe.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">When answering questions about diabetes, be precise about the cause: Type 1 is 'the body cannot produce insulin' and Type 2 is 'the body does not produce enough insulin or the insulin does not work properly'. Do not say Type 2 is simply 'diet-related' — this is too vague. For hypoglycaemia questions, state the treatment as 'fast-acting glucose / a sugary drink', not just 'eat something'. Accuracy on medical conditions is directly linked to marks.</p>

