=== L9 Common Medical Conditions: Epilepsy & Sudden Cardiac Arrest ===
id: cd0baa92-ea01-47a4-a046-9b60d0442355
slug: lesson-09
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:51:15.58557+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Causes, symptoms and treatment of epilepsy and sudden cardiac arrest, including AED use, CPR and cardiac arrest vs.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Two Serious Conditions in Sport</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Epilepsy and sudden cardiac arrest (SCA) are two of the most serious medical conditions a coach or sports practitioner may encounter. Both can be life-threatening in a sporting context. Understanding the causes, recognising the symptoms and knowing the correct response can make the difference between life and death. This lesson covers both conditions in the depth required by the specification.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Epilepsy: Overview and Causes</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4"><dfn class="term" data-def="A neurological condition characterised by recurring seizures caused by abnormal electrical activity in the brain.">Epilepsy</dfn> is a neurological condition in which abnormal bursts of electrical activity in the brain cause recurrent <dfn class="term" data-def="Episodes of uncontrolled electrical activity in the brain that temporarily affect movement, sensation, consciousness or behaviour.">seizures</dfn>. Not all seizures are the dramatic convulsions often associated with epilepsy — they range from brief lapses in consciousness to full body shaking. Three key triggers are listed in the specification: <strong>severe head injuries</strong> (which can damage brain tissue and disrupt normal electrical patterns), <strong>anxiety or stress</strong> (psychological states that can lower the seizure threshold in susceptible individuals) and <strong>tiredness or lack of sleep</strong> (fatigue reduces the brain's resilience to abnormal electrical activity). In sport, these triggers are all plausible — contact sport carries head injury risk, competition brings stress, and overtrained athletes may be sleep-deprived.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Epilepsy: Symptoms of Seizures</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Seizure symptoms affect different parts of the body. In the <strong>eyes</strong>, a performer may stare blankly or show flickering/fluttering of the eyelids — this may look like a brief lapse of attention or a temporary absence. In the <strong>mouth</strong>, symptoms include biting the tongue, making random involuntary noises or vocalisation, or producing excessive saliva. In the <strong>limbs</strong>, seizures may cause stiffness (tonic phase) followed by rhythmic jerking movements of the arms and legs (clonic phase), or sudden loss of muscle tone causing the performer to collapse to the ground.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n7" data-revision-tip="The three triggers for epilepsy are severe head injuries, anxiety/stress and tiredness/lack of sleep. Learn the three body areas affected during a seizure — eyes, mouth, limbs — and give one example symptom for each. This is frequently tested as a describe question.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Epilepsy seizure symptoms affect: eyes (staring blankly, fluttering), mouth (biting tongue, random noises) and limbs (stiffness and jerking movements). The three main triggers in sport are severe head injuries, anxiety/stress and tiredness/lack of sleep.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Epilepsy: Treatment</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">The two main treatments for epilepsy are <strong>anti-epileptic drugs (AEDs)</strong> — medication taken regularly to reduce the frequency and severity of seizures by stabilising the brain's electrical activity — and the <strong>ketogenic diet</strong>, a high-fat, very low-carbohydrate diet that has been shown to reduce seizure frequency in some patients, particularly children, when medication is not fully effective. In a sporting context, if a performer has a seizure, the immediate response is to protect them from injury (move hard objects away, cushion the head), do not restrain them, place them in the recovery position once convulsions stop, stay with them and call 999 if the seizure lasts more than 5 minutes or they do not regain consciousness.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Managing Epilepsy During Sport: Practical Guidance</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">A performer with epilepsy should inform their coach and ensure the coaching team know their seizure plan. Sports that involve heights, open water or fast-moving equipment carry additional risk and may require adapted supervision or individual risk assessments. During a seizure, never put anything in the performer's mouth (contrary to myth, swallowing the tongue is not possible). Head protection and the avoidance of severe head trauma are important preventive measures given the link between head injury and seizure triggers.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Sudden Cardiac Arrest: Overview and Causes</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12"><dfn class="term" data-def="A medical emergency where the heart suddenly stops beating effectively, causing loss of consciousness and cessation of normal breathing.">Sudden cardiac arrest (SCA)</dfn> occurs when the heart's electrical system malfunctions, causing it to stop pumping blood. Without immediate treatment, brain damage begins within 4–6 minutes and death within 10 minutes. Three causes are listed in the specification: <strong>underlying genetic heart conditions</strong> — such as hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM), an inherited thickening of the heart muscle that is the most common cause of SCA in young athletes; <strong>intense physical activity</strong> — which can trigger SCA in those with undetected heart conditions by placing extreme demand on an already abnormal heart; and <strong>sudden trauma</strong> — a severe blow to the chest at a specific moment in the cardiac cycle (called commotio cordis) can trigger ventricular fibrillation.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">SCA vs Heart Attack: A Critical Distinction</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">The specification requires you to know the difference between cardiac arrest and a heart attack. A <strong>heart attack</strong> (myocardial infarction) is caused by a blockage in a coronary artery — the heart muscle is starved of blood but continues to beat. The performer is typically conscious, may experience crushing chest pain, breathlessness and pain radiating to the arm or jaw, and can often speak. A <strong>sudden cardiac arrest</strong> is an electrical failure — the heart stops beating effectively. The performer is unconscious and not breathing normally. Both are emergencies requiring 999, but SCA additionally requires immediate CPR and defibrillation to have any chance of survival.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n15" data-revision-tip="Learn the distinction: heart attack = blockage (heart still beats, performer conscious); cardiac arrest = electrical failure (heart stops, performer unconscious, not breathing). Examiners will test this in a 'compare' question.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Sudden cardiac arrest: the heart stops beating (unconscious, not breathing normally) — requires CPR and a defibrillator immediately. Heart attack: a blocked coronary artery starves heart muscle of blood but the heart keeps beating — the performer is conscious. Both require 999 but only SCA requires CPR and defibrillation.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">SCA Treatment: CPR and Defibrillation</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">The symptoms of SCA are: <strong>unconsciousness</strong> (sudden collapse with no response) and <strong>breathing difficulties</strong> (agonal breathing — irregular gasping — or complete absence of breathing). Treatment follows the DRABC protocol. Once SCA is suspected, call 999 immediately and begin <dfn class="term" data-def="An emergency technique involving chest compressions and rescue breaths to manually circulate blood and oxygen when the heart has stopped.">CPR</dfn> — 30 chest compressions to 2 rescue breaths, at a rate of 100–120 compressions per minute, pushing down 5–6 cm on the centre of the chest. A <dfn class="term" data-def="An Automated External Defibrillator: a portable device that delivers an electric shock to restore normal heart rhythm during cardiac arrest.">defibrillator (AED)</dfn> should be retrieved and used as soon as possible — the AED analyses the heart rhythm and delivers a controlled electric shock if ventricular fibrillation is detected, which can restore a normal rhythm. Every minute without defibrillation reduces survival chances by approximately 10%. Lifestyle changes — including maintaining a healthy weight, not smoking, and regular cardiovascular exercise — reduce the long-term risk of cardiac events.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Mistake: Confusing AED Medication with Anti-Epileptic Drugs</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">In the epilepsy section, the specification abbreviates anti-epileptic drugs as 'AEDs'. In the cardiac arrest section, 'AED' means Automated External Defibrillator. These are two completely different things. In an exam, always write the full term first — 'anti-epileptic drugs (AEDs)' or 'Automated External Defibrillator (AED)' — and then use the abbreviation, so the examiner can be certain which you mean. Confusing the two would result in a medically incorrect answer and lost marks.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Epilepsy seizures are triggered by severe head injuries, anxiety/stress and tiredness; symptoms affect the eyes (staring, fluttering), mouth (biting tongue, random noises) and limbs (stiffness and jerking); treatment is anti-epileptic drugs or a ketogenic diet.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Sudden cardiac arrest occurs when the heart stops beating, causing unconsciousness and abnormal or absent breathing; it must be distinguished from a heart attack, where the heart still beats and the performer remains conscious.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Treatment for SCA requires immediate 999 call, CPR (30 chest compressions to 2 rescue breaths) and an AED (Automated External Defibrillator) as soon as possible; every minute without defibrillation reduces survival odds by approximately 10%.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">For epilepsy questions, use the specification's exact body-area framework for symptoms: eyes, mouth, limbs — then give one example for each. For SCA questions, be precise about the treatment sequence: call 999, begin CPR (30:2), retrieve and use AED. Do not say 'give CPR' without specifying the compression-to-breath ratio. Always state the key distinction: in cardiac arrest the performer is unconscious and not breathing; in a heart attack they are conscious. This distinction is worth at least 1 mark in any comparison question.</p>

