  "marks": "Award up to 3 marks. Basic distinction (1 mark): in a heart attack the heart keeps beating; in cardiac arrest it stops. Development of heart attack (1 mark): a heart attack is caused by a blockage in a coronary artery that stops blood reaching part of the heart muscle — the performer is typically conscious and may feel crushing chest pain. Development of cardiac arrest (1 mark): sudden cardiac arrest is an electrical failure of the heart — the heart stops pumping effectively, the performer is unconscious and not breathing normally. [3 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "Describe how a first aider should treat a young athlete who collapses during a race and is suspected to be in sudden cardiac arrest. Give an example of how the AED would be used.",
  "type": "4 marks — Describe with Example",
  "marks": "Award up to 4 marks. Call 999 immediately (1 mark). Begin CPR — 30 chest compressions (5–6 cm deep, centre of chest) to 2 rescue breaths, at 100–120 compressions per minute (1 mark). Retrieve the AED as soon as possible and switch it on (1 mark). AED example (1 mark): the AED gives voice instructions — attach the pads to the bare chest as shown, stand clear while it analyses the heart rhythm, and deliver the shock if prompted; resume CPR immediately after the shock and continue until paramedics arrive. [4 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "A swimming club coach is concerned about the risk of medical emergencies during competitive galas attended by up to 200 spectators and 80 swimmers.\n\nDiscuss the measures the club should take to manage the risk of epileptic seizures and sudden cardiac arrest during competitive events.",
  "type": "6 marks — Discuss",
  "marks": "Mastering (5–6 marks): A structured discussion of measures for both conditions. For epilepsy: pre-event disclosure of seizure plans, adapted supervision near water, designated first aider who knows the protocol, what to do during a seizure (do not restrain, protect from injury, recovery position, 999 if over 5 minutes). For SCA: AED on site and accessible, staff CPR-trained, Emergency Action Plan with named personnel and communication plan, screening of performers. At least one contrast — e.g. having equipment and plans is essential but only effective if staff are trained and confident to use them.\nSecure (3–4 marks): Measures for both conditions described with some explanation.\nDeveloping (2 marks): One condition covered adequately, the other only briefly.\nEmerging (1 mark): One relevant safety measure. [6 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "Every year, young athletes suffer sudden cardiac arrest during sport. Many do not survive because bystanders do not act quickly enough or the right equipment is not available.\n\nEvaluate the extent to which having trained staff and defibrillators at all sporting venues would reduce deaths from sudden cardiac arrest in sport.",
  "type": "8 marks — Evaluate (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering (7–8 marks): A balanced evaluation arguing that trained staff and on-site AEDs significantly improve survival rates (evidence: survival rates can exceed 70% when CPR and defibrillation are started within 3–5 minutes, compared to less than 10% when emergency services arrive first). Counter-arguments should include: cost and maintenance of AEDs, need for regular retraining, the fact that most cardiac arrests still occur without warning despite screening, and that lifestyle changes and pre-participation screening may be more preventive. A clear, substantiated judgement is reached about the relative importance of different strategies.\nSecure (5–6 marks): Both sides explored with some application; partial judgement.\nDeveloping (3–4 marks): Mainly lists benefits without counter-argument or clear judgement.\nEmerging (1–2 marks): Basic points with no evaluation. [8 marks]"
 }
]

